cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: >-
  tlecheck: screening a two-line element set before propagation, with a
  measured-consequence classification and a first-class statement of what the
  screen cannot determine
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-10-29
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  Screens a two-line element set for format and checksum validity, epoch
  staleness against a declared requirement, element plausibility against bounds
  from orbital mechanics and against the object's own element history, ephemeris
  continuity against its own predecessor, and cross-source disagreement; and
  classifies each problem as one the propagator will report loudly, one that
  will produce a confidently wrong ephemeris, or one that is benign. The
  classification is a measured consequence rather than an assumed label: the
  class boundary comes from propagating the element set and its uncorrupted
  reference with sgp4 over the same grid and differencing the positions. The
  first output of every call is a report of eleven things the screen cannot
  determine, computed before any screen runs; three are structural and no amount
  of data moves them. Integrity and consequence are separate outputs, because a
  checksum says a character changed and nothing about what the change costs:
  measured over 420 objects, all 31 element sets whose only corruption is the
  checksum column have a propagated position error of exactly 0.0000 km, while
  the checksum screen detects every one with a precision and recall of 1.0000
  and no false positives in 363. The checksum's blindness is exact: it detects
  0.888889 of two-column digit substitutions and 0.000000 of digit
  transpositions, enumerated over 91,368 and 992 cases and confirmed against a
  closed form of 72/81 derived by a congruence argument that enumerates nothing.
  Validation is level 2. A single flipped drag-term sign, with a valid checksum
  and every element inside every physical bound, is worth 1.8993 km after one
  day, 93.0648 km after seven and 1704.5723 km after thirty. Four results are
  unfavourable to this package and are published as measured. The product
  specification predicted that the explicit rule screens would match or beat the
  learned classifier on every class they cover, and on a 420-object held-out
  split they do not: the learned model reaches a silent-class recall of 0.7946
  at a false-positive rate of 0.0455 where the swept rule baseline reaches
  0.4286, and it takes three of six per-class metrics against the rules' two.
  The baseline was strengthened five times before that was published and the
  margin fell from about +0.78 recall, a build-time figure labelled as not
  reproducible from the shipped code, to the +0.3661 reported here. The rules keep what the model
  cannot provide: an exact integrity verdict, a loud-failure recall of 0.9444
  from a check against a number rather than an estimate, and a win of +0.2153
  and +0.1463 of area on two corruption families withheld from the model's
  training. The learned model answers confidently where the analytic screen
  withholds its test, with 7 of 32 errors on the unresolvable subset carrying a
  decision margin above 0.5 against 0 of 11 on the resolvable one. Four of six
  orbit families cannot be screened for a one-kilometre consequence by comparing
  element sets at all, with resolutions from 1.7898 km to 29.4676 km, and the
  screen reports that rather than returning a verdict that is noise. The
  cross-source screen's precision is capped at 0.8889 by a symmetry it cannot
  break. Seventeen controls were run, twelve of which must fire and twelve of
  which did, five of which must not and none of which did. All numbers are
  measured against a seeded synthetic element catalogue whose generator is
  documented in DATASET_CARD.md and whose element histories are built with the
  SGP4 propagator itself; no flight data and no element catalogue is used or
  shipped anywhere, and the authoritative element source is a catalogue this
  product does not ship. A passing screen is not a statement that a propagated
  ephemeris is accurate. Research grade. Not flight-qualified, not certified,
  not approved for operational aerospace use.
authors:
  - name: "OPTIMA Organisation"
keywords:
  - two-line-element
  - tle
  - sgp4
  - orbit-determination
  - space-situational-awareness
  - data-quality
  - aerospace-gnc
references:
  - type: report
    authors:
      - family-names: Hoots
        given-names: "F. R."
      - family-names: Roehrich
        given-names: "R. L."
    title: "Models for Propagation of NORAD Element Sets"
    number: "Spacetrack Report No. 3"
    institution:
      name: "U.S. Air Force Aerospace Defense Command"
    year: 1980
    notes: >-
      The element definitions, the mean-element interpretation, and the
      modulo-10 digit-sum checksum rule of tlecheck.elements. That rule is
      verified in validate_elements.py against a published element set whose
      stated checksums are 1 and 2 and whose computed checksums are 1 and 2.
  - type: conference-paper
    authors:
      - family-names: Vallado
        given-names: "D. A."
      - family-names: Crawford
        given-names: "P."
      - family-names: Hujsak
        given-names: "R."
      - family-names: Kelso
        given-names: "T. S."
    title: "Revisiting Spacetrack Report #3"
    collection-title: "AIAA/AAS Astrodynamics Specialist Conference"
    number: "AIAA 2006-6753"
    year: 2006
    notes: >-
      The paper the installed sgp4 distribution implements. Section 2 for the
      column layout of the distributed text form, the implied-decimal
      exponential fields and the 1957-2056 two-digit epoch year window; section
      3 for the WGS-72 constants this package adopts; and the initl subroutine
      for the un-Kozai transformation that is equation (18) of tlecheck.dataset.
      The error codes 1 to 6 of sgp4.api.SGP4_ERRORS are this paper's.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Vallado
        given-names: "D. A."
    title: "Fundamentals of Astrodynamics and Applications"
    edition: "4th"
    publisher:
      name: "Microcosm Press"
    year: 2013
    notes: >-
      Equation (1-29) for the Kepler inversion that is equation (1); equations
      (1-16) and (1-17) for the perigee and apogee radii of equation (2);
      algorithm 14, equation (3-27), for the Julian date of equation (5), whose
      declared validity range of 1900 March 1 to 2100 February 28 was verified
      and whose one-day failure outside it is asserted as a test; and equations
      (9-38) and (9-39) for the first-order J2 secular rates of equations (16)
      and (17), which agree with the propagator's own nodedot and argpdot to a
      worst relative difference of 0.001579 and 0.279937.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Kozai
        given-names: "Y."
    title: "The motion of a close earth satellite"
    journal: "Astronomical Journal"
    volume: 64
    year: 1959
    notes: >-
      The mean motion written in a two-line element set is a Kozai mean motion.
      Writing a Brouwer mean motion into that field instead biases the
      semi-major axis by about half a kilometre, which this repository measured
      as 112.09 km of along-track drift two and a half days later.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Brouwer
        given-names: "D."
    title: >-
      Solution of the problem of artificial satellite theory without drag
    journal: "Astronomical Journal"
    volume: 64
    year: 1959
    notes: >-
      The theory whose mean motion the propagator works with internally, and
      the other end of the transformation of equation (18).
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Rousseeuw
        given-names: "P. J."
      - family-names: Croux
        given-names: "C."
    title: "Alternatives to the median absolute deviation"
    journal: "Journal of the American Statistical Association"
    volume: 88
    issue: 424
    year: 1993
    notes: >-
      Section 2, the 1.4826 consistency constant making a scaled median absolute
      deviation an unbiased estimator of the standard deviation for Gaussian
      data. It is equation (7) and it is used by the history screen and by the
      continuity screen. Its small-sample variance over twelve points is why
      the measured per-element false-positive rate at a four-sigma threshold is
      0.0433 rather than 0.0001, which this repository reports rather than
      corrects.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Sen
        given-names: "P. K."
    title: >-
      Estimates of the regression coefficient based on Kendall's tau
    journal: "Journal of the American Statistical Association"
    volume: 63
    issue: 324
    year: 1968
    notes: >-
      Equation (2.6), the median-of-pairwise-slopes estimator that is equation
      (9), and section 5 for the median intercept paired with it. Used for the
      three elements that genuinely drift: the node, the mean motion and the
      drag term.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Theil
        given-names: "H."
    title: >-
      A rank-invariant method of linear and polynomial regression analysis
    journal: >-
      Proceedings of the Royal Netherlands Academy of Sciences
    volume: 53
    year: 1950
    notes: >-
      The estimator Sen (1968) builds on.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Hamilton
        given-names: "D. P."
      - family-names: Burns
        given-names: "J. A."
    title: "Orbital stability zones about asteroids"
    journal: "Icarus"
    volume: 92
    year: 1992
    notes: >-
      The form of the Hill-sphere stability radius behind equation (4). The
      numerical value used is the standard Earth figure of about 1.5e6 km and it
      is used only as an order-of-magnitude ceiling on the apogee a geocentric
      two-body element set can describe.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Pedregosa
        given-names: "F."
      - family-names: Varoquaux
        given-names: "G."
      - family-names: Gramfort
        given-names: "A."
    title: "Scikit-learn: machine learning in Python"
    journal: "Journal of Machine Learning Research"
    volume: 12
    year: 2011
    notes: >-
      RandomForestClassifier, whose per-member probabilities tlecheck.learned
      reads to produce the ensemble dispersion of equation (19). That read is
      why scikit-learn is pinned below 1.10.
  - type: software
    authors:
      - name: "Brandon Rhodes and the sgp4 contributors"
    title: "sgp4"
    version: "2.27"
    notes: >-
      The propagator. Every consequence figure in this repository was produced
      by it, every secular-rate and semi-major-axis cross-check is against its
      internals, and this package screens for it and does not replace it. Two
      measured behaviours of it motivate this package and neither is a defect
      in it: it does not verify the element set checksum, and two lines of 69
      spaces parse to object 0 with a mean motion of 0.0. tlecheck.dataset also
      reads its post-propagation mean elements nm, em, im, Om, om and mm, which
      are an implementation surface rather than a promised API, which is why the
      dependency is pinned below 2.28.
